    <div class="lux-reader-rule"></div>
    <section class="lux-reader-hero"><div class="lux-reader-hero-copy"><div class="lux-reader-meta-grid"><span>Italian Food Dictionary</span><span>Italian Food Dictionary · 2026</span></div><h1 id="lux-reader-title">Italian Cheese and Cured Meat Dictionary</h1><p class="lux-reader-summary">Italian cheeses and cured meats carry many regional names. These names are not simply brands. They can describe different ingredients, production methods, ageing periods, and territories.</p></div><figure class="lux-reader-cover"><img width="2000" height="1334" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" src="<?php echo esc_url(get_template_directory_uri() . '/assets/media/academy/dictionary-cheese-cured-meat.webp'); ?>" alt="Italian Cheese and Cured Meat Dictionary"></figure></section>
    <section class="lux-reader-content"><aside class="lux-reader-aside"><a href="<?php echo esc_url(luxureat_static_url('en/blog', '')); ?>">Back to Knowledge Blog</a></aside><div class="lux-reader-copy"><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-0"><h2>Parmigiano Reggiano</h2><p>Parmigiano Reggiano is a protected Italian hard cheese produced according to a specific geographical and production specification.</p><p>It is commonly used:</p><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>grated over pasta;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>in risotto;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>in soups;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>in pieces as a table cheese;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>with other regional ingredients.</li></ul><p>Not every hard grated cheese should automatically be called Parmigiano Reggiano.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-1"><h2>Grana Padano</h2><p>Grana Padano is another protected Italian hard cheese.</p><p>It can resemble Parmigiano Reggiano in appearance and certain culinary uses, but they are not the same product.</p><p>Their production territories and specifications differ.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-2"><h2>Mozzarella</h2><p>Mozzarella belongs to the family of fresh stretched-curd cheeses.</p><p>Different versions exist, including cow&#39;s-milk mozzarella and buffalo-milk mozzarella.</p><p>Common uses include:</p><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>pizza;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>salads;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>starters;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>baked dishes.</li></ul></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-3"><h2>Burrata</h2><p>Burrata has an outer structure similar to stretched-curd cheese, but contains a soft, creamy interior.</p><p>When cut, the centre is noticeably richer and more fluid than ordinary mozzarella.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-4"><h2>Pecorino</h2><p>The word pecorino is linked to the Italian word for sheep.</p><p>Pecorino therefore generally refers to sheep&#39;s-milk cheese.</p><p>Important examples include:</p><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>Pecorino Romano;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>Pecorino Toscano;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>Pecorino Sardo.</li></ul><p>Flavour and saltiness can differ significantly depending on origin and maturation.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-5"><h2>Prosciutto</h2><p>Prosciutto means ham.</p><p>The next word tells you more about what kind of ham it is.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-6"><h2>Prosciutto Crudo</h2><p>Prosciutto crudo is salt-cured and matured without being cooked.</p><p>The term “raw” describes the production process and should not be confused with untreated fresh pork.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-7"><h2>Prosciutto Cotto</h2><p>Prosciutto cotto is cooked ham.</p><p>It differs significantly from prosciutto crudo in colour, texture, and flavour.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-8"><h2>Prosciutto di Parma</h2><p>Prosciutto di Parma is a specific protected product from the Parma production area.</p><p>“Italian cured ham” and “Prosciutto di Parma” are therefore not interchangeable terms.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-9"><h2>Salame</h2><p>Salame refers to a broad family of seasoned and matured meat products.</p><p>Italy has many regional types, differing in meat texture, seasoning, size, and maturation.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-10"><h2>LuxurEat(露意膳) Perspective</h2><p>LuxurEat(露意膳) prefers to preserve authentic Italian food names rather than reducing everything to generic terms such as “cheese” or “ham.”</p><p>Once consumers understand whether a product is Parmigiano Reggiano, Pecorino, Burrata, or Prosciutto di Parma, they can also understand how it should be served and why its texture, flavour, and price may differ.</p><p>The Italian Food Dictionary begins with the name, but its real purpose is to help consumers understand the product behind the name.</p></section></div></section>
